    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d435">

                <pb facs="0663" n="630" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v23/pg630"/>

                <head>435. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in Belgium<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d435-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1965-10-14T10:00:00-04:00">October 14, 1965,
                            10 a.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>436. Request you consult soonest with appropriate GOB officials, preferably Spaak if health and schedule permit, to determine Belgian views
                    re significance and consequences Kasavubu’s dismissal of Tshombe.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d435-f2"/> What US-Belgian action
                    indicated? Can we make effort re-establish Kasavubu–Tshombe tandem
                    and, if so, by what means?</p>
                <p>Harriman will wish exchange views and
                    explore with Spaak next week possible
                    joint or parallel actions in light situation which we hope will then be somewhat
                    clearer. If Belgians believe action required before that time, advise
                        soonest.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d435-f3"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>Rusk</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d435-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d435-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files
                    1964–66, POL 23–9 THE CONGO. Secret. Drafted by McElhiney and approved by
                        Williams. Also sent to
                    Leopoldville.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d435-f2" n="2" xml:id="d435-f2-content">
                <p>On October 13, President Kasavubu
                    dismissed Tshombe and appointed Evariste
                        Kimba as Prime Minister.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d435-f3" n="3" xml:id="d435-f3-content">
                <p>Telegram 427 from Brussels, October 15, reported that Spaak and the Belgian Government were
                    convinced that Belgium should not intervene in the current Congolese political
                    maneuverings. (National Archives, RG 59, Central
                    Files 1964–66, POL 23–9 THE CONGO)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
